[{"id":3455793140,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOAO6SJc7N-zf0","state":"APPROVED","user":{"site_admin":false,"suspended":false,"updated_at":"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/180032?u=68d23e7ee9cdf810da125e8510b72f845b9dccc8&v=4","created_at":"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z","followers":0,"following":0,"html_url":"https://github.com/Calinou","id":180032,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4MDAzMg==","login":"Calinou","type":"User","owned_private_repos":0,"public_gists":0,"public_repos":0,"total_private_repos":0,"url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou"},"body":"Tested locally, it works as expected. Code looks good to me.\r\n\r\nI don't mind the potential for false positives personally. In terms of overall usability when working with complex strings, the upsides greatly outweigh the downsides. Visual Studio Code does the same in Python:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"238\" height=\"55\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cef6bd92-d952-40e1-8f97-55f11ace770d\" />\r\n\r\nThe default color looks good to me on both dark and light theme, although it's a little hard to distinguish from regular text within a StringName. That said, using a placeholder within a StringName is very rare (and arguably a sign that you shouldn't be using a StringName in the first place), so it's probably fine.\r\n\r\nDefault | Light\r\n-|-\r\n<img width=\"325\" height=\"87\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a88e8b9c-52dd-4da5-a0ec-a6df8af1ac94\" /> | <img width=\"325\" height=\"87\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/dd0b35e9-3be3-4926-8ca4-b9d4ec2ef2d5\" />\r\n","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/112575#pullrequestreview-3455793140","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/112575","author_association":"MEMBER","submitted_at":"2025-11-12T22:39:28Z"},{"id":3439652063,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOAO6SJc7NBOzf","state":"APPROVED","user":{"site_admin":false,"suspended":false,"up